[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02709403-b870-584e-98ba-6c863f227d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321170} {:answer/id #uuid "0b11217e-c071-5ec6-ac11-eab13c676a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321237} {:answer/id #uuid "23b68c39-1ad8-5ba8-8266-734fae77a185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321420} {:answer/id #uuid "75a44b6e-9f06-5fb9-95f2-f4dc45a9d9d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322064} {:answer/id #uuid "ebec7e42-ccf2-59d1-a11a-4093a463037c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02709403-b870-584e-98ba-6c863f227d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321170} :answer-field/id #uuid "11c47907-1c5c-5193-9fe8-a1540f384554" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f50f445-3873-5d48-8eec-e37880dda36a" :question/math-academy-id "q-35513" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{4}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8afe1cc-6a73-59a7-a3aa-86abc400ccd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}(4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8afe1cc-6a73-59a7-a3aa-86abc400ccd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}(4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322470} :answer-field/id #uuid "549d1fa9-dbbb-5a28-9ec0-95d21eef029c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a3b691d-ce23-5509-8fc2-d490e3df8940" :question/math-academy-id "q-355525" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y){y}^{′}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=0$ that satisfies $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=0.$ Assume that $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})+C \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ 0 & =0+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90c5bd0e-992a-5060-a1b1-7adb29cef3ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2}+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90c5bd0e-992a-5060-a1b1-7adb29cef3ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2}+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322298} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f6b0da-0d33-5c51-ac9b-a4198b00fb73" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "805542c0-bca8-5f60-bf27-701c39eb6996" :question/math-academy-id "q-355576" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x-y}$ given that  $\\,y(0)=2.$\n\n$y(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-2x-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-2x}{e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{0}+C) & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-\\frac{1}{2}) & =2 \\\\ C-\\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{2{e}^{2}+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x-y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=2$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{-{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2}+1}{2}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a653501-7f39-554f-8700-17ab8e35a4da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a653501-7f39-554f-8700-17ab8e35a4da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322252} :answer-field/id #uuid "10c651f7-74fe-5f62-ad22-da3d8a63750e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44820654-043f-529e-8512-cf2fa8eaf9b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-356029" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}-3$ and $y=1.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](images/66/664fcc791ec42b4ec40ed3fc7033fa601d646c9900632864122d31fd01a2cf61.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/1d/1d10b1994774239767578417bc5f0b74a6a7369005a73fbac859574c9d5d36e0.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-3,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(1)-({x}^{2}-3)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(4x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =[4\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3}]-[4\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-2)^{3}] \\\\  & =(8-\\frac{8}{3})-(-8+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-(-\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acc15cc8-16fd-50fa-8523-a77c71d90858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acc15cc8-16fd-50fa-8523-a77c71d90858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322500} :answer-field/id #uuid "2032fb96-09c8-5ded-b5bd-deeda3577ddf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323229} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d38fff03-6da9-5ce6-a633-7885919027e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d38fff03-6da9-5ce6-a633-7885919027e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322799} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0b201f2-c9f2-5028-a916-abafa9097eaa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "587316a5-f847-5f83-912c-b52ebafdaeb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-357255" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t) & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02365f64-5f8c-566c-a522-523a978e5e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321168} {:answer/id #uuid "8015e52d-c714-5fad-9fc0-fc9ff19ddbbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322152} {:answer/id #uuid "8c323b73-baef-507e-917b-e46dc735a5b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322268} {:answer/id #uuid "d673765d-648f-52bf-8827-db6da877b49c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322821} {:answer/id #uuid "fb040589-7b1a-5f83-9142-68e2dc95668b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb040589-7b1a-5f83-9142-68e2dc95668b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323126} :answer-field/id #uuid "d44c3910-a676-5e6f-b07c-e38c6a923181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d22c3d57-064f-5a29-85f5-aa8e18bd8665" :question/math-academy-id "q-35830" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{9{x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}+11}{12{x}^{5}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}+11}{12{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{9{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{11}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{12{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(9+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{x}^{5}})}{(12)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+0+0}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16123c5b-0e24-5448-94af-edee5d7a2379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321327} {:answer/id #uuid "54a80482-3b1f-51c4-b743-a176cf719e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321813} {:answer/id #uuid "570b242a-d6a5-5843-b3d2-25a379d35ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321834} {:answer/id #uuid "6575a5ba-b2fa-552c-a16a-377b10470e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321933} {:answer/id #uuid "af8740d1-b898-5366-95a3-967eda7c4f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "570b242a-d6a5-5843-b3d2-25a379d35ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321834} :answer-field/id #uuid "0795df74-4d2f-57e9-b4f9-ecdee6b9ed2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a34c28ce-0cb3-5e56-ab36-3c5183b046d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35831" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{6{x}^{3}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{6{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14}{(6+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{6+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=\\frac{7}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a83d4ad1-4592-577a-9a6b-f5fa68bf90b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a83d4ad1-4592-577a-9a6b-f5fa68bf90b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322464} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f29d86e-519b-56da-bf1c-57233f56e8b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "615eeedf-3ba4-59d8-9d3a-634f4529d73f" :question/math-academy-id "q-358782" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,12t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$\n{{field-1}}$,0\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,12⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25(1)+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,12⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{12}{13}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{12}{13})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{13}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{169}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{5}{13}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{5}{13})}=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5871484e-21bb-5092-b09c-e08bf26d1e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321842} {:answer/id #uuid "a87dc9d9-165f-54c0-8d1c-b67b7c6e81f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a87dc9d9-165f-54c0-8d1c-b67b7c6e81f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442589} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9dd7521-e5a8-502d-93ab-04dd55f565ed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e77a13f7-0f57-5d23-af8b-ebbc48aa1422" :question/math-academy-id "q-358783" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$\n{{field-1}}$,0\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c0c3933-f2eb-5430-9130-c222c7dd3385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321486} {:answer/id #uuid "2dced506-c98b-54f2-b521-a33b0d31abca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321505} {:answer/id #uuid "80578236-5da7-59f5-bd63-88412bcc2467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{cot}^{-1}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322155} {:answer/id #uuid "b8492909-14e1-53f7-8fa9-31eb444b5dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322580} {:answer/id #uuid "f8841ffb-1d88-5c6c-a30f-dff18090dbb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{cot}^{-1}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dced506-c98b-54f2-b521-a33b0d31abca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321505} :answer-field/id #uuid "be8e3c92-c6bd-5872-9009-f104c52acf8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ddbb116-5940-52b1-bf90-b7efeb6e8633" :question/math-academy-id "q-3588" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-8x+20}\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+20 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+({4}^{2}-{4}^{2})+20 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2})-16+20 \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-8x+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-4)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x-4}{2}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x-4}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-8x+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-4)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bb97f34-32f2-5fbb-aec5-57ace0fbaad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321872} {:answer/id #uuid "8b638a94-901f-5e77-bbce-f68bb55836a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x-4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322260} {:answer/id #uuid "c656713f-8a67-5fd6-8734-5401564b9a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x+4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322683} {:answer/id #uuid "d689d4b8-8b89-5de7-b04a-9ad318e85b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322822} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7edee3-ab4b-5148-91d5-eeb3f01579ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c656713f-8a67-5fd6-8734-5401564b9a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x+4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322683} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a9b73dc-645a-581e-bb60-7a672cb6e2c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84868fd8-3ce0-5bf2-96b8-b86160226a86" :question/math-academy-id "q-3594" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-8x-15}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-8x-15 & =-({x}^{2}+8x+15) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+[{4}^{2}-{4}^{2}]+15) \\\\  & =-((x+4)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-8x-15}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+4).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-8x-15}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x+4)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f9fc2f6-55bd-579f-8d29-1c23432bc9d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321268} {:answer/id #uuid "60e28288-b23d-5dcd-b0df-f485ee49c892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321907} {:answer/id #uuid "67ff6316-9900-5d46-835b-dc3f73fa9490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321959} {:answer/id #uuid "8b7eefa4-73e4-5852-8337-87a7151461a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322262} {:answer/id #uuid "e6558f18-e9d0-5aff-84ab-a80892c690b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f9fc2f6-55bd-579f-8d29-1c23432bc9d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321268} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c408a79-7d5b-5c64-bf80-a6ec1301f01a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4dc17c5e-dc05-517c-b33a-803dd069587a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36009" :question/problem "Evaluate the gradient of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0)$ if $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),\\,{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟨x,\\,y⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\,0) & =\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{0}^{2}}⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\,\\frac{1}{4}}⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =8⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f1767a3-1f5e-5513-8f07-b57db720be84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|-\\frac{3}{x}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321265} {:answer/id #uuid "3cbb9d43-569b-505a-964a-5abffc82feed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321618} {:answer/id #uuid "87335f01-34a2-5e0e-84bd-153053b9b954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}|-\\frac{1}{x}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322220} {:answer/id #uuid "87850f46-b969-54a6-9efc-aa99c23f6b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{3}|+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5{x}^{2}|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322224} {:answer/id #uuid "9e54ad9a-e9f2-5760-9f4c-cf94a74c6cfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e54ad9a-e9f2-5760-9f4c-cf94a74c6cfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322394} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1f868f3-e53c-5bf4-ae3e-d2c4e263b9fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c90ef52c-ba49-546e-a3d2-a0b1595aa340" :question/math-academy-id "q-3609" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{2}(x-5)}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{2}(x-5)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-5)}.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by ${x}^{2}(x-5)$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x-5=A(x)(x-5)+B(x-5)+C{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=5,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({5}^{2})-2(5)-5 & =A(5)(5-5)+B(5-5)+C(5)^{2} \\\\ 10 & =C(25) \\\\ C & =\\frac{10}{25} \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}-2(0)-5 & =A(0)(0-5)+B(0-5)+C(0)^{2} \\\\ -5 & =B(-5) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-5}{-5} \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({1}^{2})-2(1)-5 & =A(1)(1-5)+B(1-5)+C(1)^{2} \\\\ -6 & =-4A-4B+C \\\\ -6 & =-4A-4(1)+(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ 4A & =6-4+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 4A & =\\frac{10+2}{5} \\\\ A & =\\frac{12}{20} \\\\ A & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{2}(x-5)}=\\frac{3}{5x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{5(x-5)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{2}(x-5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{5x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{5(x-5)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{x-5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323758 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a07e8c-0d67-55a0-881c-3c7ad3584a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321306} {:answer/id #uuid "143c3dc4-b275-59ab-82da-1687d8c3670b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321312} {:answer/id #uuid "408f6414-20da-5d9d-9573-95a8e2965353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321657} {:answer/id #uuid "791821d2-5503-575c-8113-5c6cf088de0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322100} {:answer/id #uuid "8cad3b73-177a-52f5-b52e-90d072312464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "143c3dc4-b275-59ab-82da-1687d8c3670b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321312} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb63bad1-66a8-55a7-a0fa-643ca9560f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96d5da43-5132-5ee1-8491-d36fb395f680" :question/math-academy-id "q-3615" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\nD=(-2)^{2}-4(1)(3)=-8<0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}+\\frac{C}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-3 & =(Ax+B)(x+1)+C({x}^{2}-2x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get  $A=\\frac{3}{2},$ $B=-\\frac{3}{2},$ and $C=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{3x-3}{2({x}^{2}-2x+3)}-\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nNow we integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration only at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3x-3}{2({x}^{2}-2x+3)}-\\frac{1}{2(x+1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}-2x+3.$ Then $(2x-2)\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}-2x+3| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5af545d0-dcb5-5648-ab4f-0b94b13d63e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+\\frac{1}{2x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321864} {:answer/id #uuid "93d40251-d875-5f5d-aef0-72ef06d9ec10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+\\frac{2}{x-1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322322} {:answer/id #uuid "d60bd1aa-c2d7-5998-9acb-c89cddd5adb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+\\frac{2}{x-1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322817} {:answer/id #uuid "e6778e04-6c08-54d2-a3df-10a120183457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322951} {:answer/id #uuid "e726d17c-3f3d-5cec-800c-b9ec3bd91662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e726d17c-3f3d-5cec-800c-b9ec3bd91662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322958} :answer-field/id #uuid "883e337e-d198-5733-933e-966f03198bed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "acee9da4-09d4-567b-82f2-79a4b4042b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-3617" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a discriminant that is not a perfect square:\n\n$$\nD={4}^{2}-4(1)(-1)=20\n$$\n\nWe have that $\\sqrt{20}$ is not a rational number, which implies that the quadratic factor doesn't factor over the rational numbers.\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+9x-4=A({x}^{2}+4x-1)+(Bx+C)(x-1)\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=1$ and $C=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}=\\frac{2}{x-1}+\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x-1}+\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+4x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n2\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+4x-1.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dae13bb9-55f3-5345-93c6-f0902c6bda50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi +4}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dae13bb9-55f3-5345-93c6-f0902c6bda50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi +4}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322860} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c8150fb-fd9e-5f6f-b6d1-de7e62140686" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e7ec77f-c052-5912-b0a1-099aaa7af7c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-361705" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(3{sin}^{2}⁡x+7{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall  ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{sin}^{2}⁡x+7{cos}^{2}⁡x & =3{sin}^{2}⁡x+3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+4{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3+4{cos}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(3+4{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[3+\\frac{4}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(5+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})]-[5\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]-[0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+1]-[0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi +4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b4b0279-8e87-5626-99b1-6e308ce668c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi -{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b4b0279-8e87-5626-99b1-6e308ce668c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi -{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322116} :answer-field/id #uuid "16d5ac7c-9c1d-52e6-a055-74d75c1afb2c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "292b6739-634d-583f-b073-f74b62cabddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-361711" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f8aa97ff1ff27ec11df6aefd4523ed9d39efac686c777f7242701aa2f4fef79c.png)\n\nA region in the $xy$-plane is bounded by the curve $y=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2-x,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, as shown above. The area of the region is\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate this integral, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)+2-x]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+3-x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )+3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}]-[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+3(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}] \\\\  & =[\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}]-[0] \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pi -{\\pi }^{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a620de-8bd0-57cc-9ca8-627d570b10b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321285} {:answer/id #uuid "539a1518-741f-5932-ab88-ffb0b81d4bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321805} {:answer/id #uuid "6d69e5bf-08b0-519c-bd8e-5f0d93d50b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321997} {:answer/id #uuid "ac78bbbd-2aa6-5d72-9963-b167b6edf486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322497} {:answer/id #uuid "c761e53e-5917-50ee-b4b8-03842b140243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac78bbbd-2aa6-5d72-9963-b167b6edf486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322497} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a86fdda-9d41-5703-bec7-0aa41e4dbc4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c3c463e-b1e6-5da9-a9e8-9ab02a63ca2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36227" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ at the point $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{x}^{2},2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa96cf1-4d31-5deb-b3f4-3a3294848a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fa96cf1-4d31-5deb-b3f4-3a3294848a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321270} :answer-field/id #uuid "17fe46a9-616b-5b04-b80d-53e10861f0fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39b5bd8a-dd36-5527-89de-e36f567f0906" :question/math-academy-id "q-362509" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)}{1-{tan}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwe compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)}{1-{tan}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)}{(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89a639e1-ea30-5852-945d-838c01f3a530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322245} {:answer/id #uuid "80e254bb-01fa-560a-b35d-95dbd95c9a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80e254bb-01fa-560a-b35d-95dbd95c9a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436409} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfbc300f-8435-5b63-80a7-df2fbc495791" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a3e4c61-3446-503d-9221-974875074079" :question/math-academy-id "q-362530" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-{cot}^{4}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity\n\n$$\n1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-{cot}^{4}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1-{cot}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323777 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb021270-a587-52a3-90c1-6e371b9245f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb021270-a587-52a3-90c1-6e371b9245f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322725} :answer-field/id #uuid "28f0eab9-3abe-5ec9-987c-6dcdda6496ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e12233f-3a34-57d8-bf38-b36992ad1033" :question/math-academy-id "q-362546" :question/problem "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}$ for $x\\in [0,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}},$ $a=0,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(2-{x}^{2})^{-1/2}(-2x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2-{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}}{2-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}))|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})]-[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{2}})] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323779 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe904164-bd58-57b3-8afe-99b03b578a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe904164-bd58-57b3-8afe-99b03b578a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323154} :answer-field/id #uuid "b444c433-7aa2-55ff-a3aa-c36421992c5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "22f34c0a-d2be-545d-8a94-5b6c02bfb19b" :question/math-academy-id "q-362589" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ for $x\\in [-1,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ $a=-1,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}=\\frac{1-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{(1-x)^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+1-2x+{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2-2x}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2(1-x)}{(1-x)(1+x)}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1+x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1+x}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1+x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{1+x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+x}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+1}-2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+(-1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}(\\sqrt{2})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6a3d9d4-e052-50e7-8d60-ebf1d18b86e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6a3d9d4-e052-50e7-8d60-ebf1d18b86e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322952} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4c1c9b8-4757-52e8-84b9-64d59325b020" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ab5bb68-6c78-5933-86bc-f3182668702a" :question/math-academy-id "q-362590" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ $a=0,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}=-\\frac{1+x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1+x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+1+2x+{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2+2x}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2(1+x)}{(1-x)(1+x)}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1-x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1-x}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1-x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1-1}-(-2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1-0}) \\\\  & =0-(-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7048b4f-eab9-53d1-89fb-1d65b5316873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{4}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7048b4f-eab9-53d1-89fb-1d65b5316873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{4}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322455} :answer-field/id #uuid "efb3e809-f5f1-5a7d-8ffc-3c402b2e5aaa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd07d931-c8c4-5e1c-9858-8aed94aa2f51" :question/math-academy-id "q-362689" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{0}12{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=-4x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}12{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =12{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{4}^{0}{e}^{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{4}^{0}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {e}^{u}\\,{|}_{4}^{0} \\\\  & =-3({e}^{0}-{e}^{4}) \\\\  & =-3(1-{e}^{4}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{4}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7841411-b2a3-5f14-a4fa-569475a21ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7841411-b2a3-5f14-a4fa-569475a21ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323091} :answer-field/id #uuid "274f1f5a-362f-555e-b4c6-9b261c2ad7ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "053d1129-5ad9-5392-8059-7eab09c97e80" :question/math-academy-id "q-362804" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{csc}^{4}⁡x-1}{{csc}^{2}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity\n\n$$\n1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{csc}^{4}⁡x-1}{{csc}^{2}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{({csc}^{2}⁡x-1)({csc}^{2}⁡x+1)}{{csc}^{2}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}({csc}^{2}⁡x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot {cot}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b38ac94-ddcc-5d36-9013-9df4a3ad1196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b38ac94-ddcc-5d36-9013-9df4a3ad1196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322115} :answer-field/id #uuid "604de9af-7501-5d6d-8f16-7e19f3aabd4c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81c9f782-ecf0-5e7b-a8aa-08e741da57f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-363485" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $f(x)={2}^{x}$ about $x=2$ to approximate the value of ${2}^{2.1}.$ Round your answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n${2}^{2.1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)={2}^{x}$ and $a=2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={2}^{x} \\\\ f(2) & =4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={2}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor expansion is\n\n$$\n{2}^{x}=4+4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)(x-2)+\\frac{4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}}{2!}(x-2)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n{2}^{x}=4+4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)(x-2)+2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}(x-2)^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=2.1.$ Since $2.1-2=0.1=\\frac{1}{10},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2.1} & ≈4+4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)(\\frac{1}{10})+2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈4.29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{2}^{2.1}≈4.29.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3add13d4-a814-5f0b-aa78-b8fbe0e663e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3add13d4-a814-5f0b-aa78-b8fbe0e663e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321599} :answer-field/id #uuid "69b10121-95e3-50b8-87ee-7d7fd482db00" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d857d55e-0426-522a-bbf3-e599533bce03" :question/math-academy-id "q-363486" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $f(x)={5}^{x}$ about $x=1$ to approximate the value of ${5}^{1.3}.$ Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\n${5}^{1.3}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)={5}^{x}$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={5}^{x} \\\\ f(1) & =5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={5}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor expansion is\n\n$$\n{5}^{x}=5+5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)(x-1)+\\frac{5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n{5}^{x}=5+5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)(x-1)+\\frac{5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}}{2}(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1.3.$ Since $1.3-1=0.3=\\frac{3}{10},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{1.3} & ≈5+5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)(\\frac{3}{10})+\\frac{5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}}{2}{(\\frac{3}{10})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈8.0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{5}^{1.3}≈8.0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a52de76-716b-5d41-98a9-3943b7c59241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a52de76-716b-5d41-98a9-3943b7c59241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321352} :answer-field/id #uuid "abecd077-f709-5b82-9c40-6c4dc262a11d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae92af54-aa04-5ea8-bfcd-575703012570" :question/math-academy-id "q-363490" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{3/t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}},\\,0<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{3/t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}})}{(-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{3/t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3/t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3({1}^{2}+4)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(5)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15\\sqrt{5}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323805 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6dcfdc0-0425-58da-9863-8d71fb334ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6dcfdc0-0425-58da-9863-8d71fb334ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322824} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ebc2e95-bd33-54eb-b38e-19227069b90a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5749738c-9ac0-51f9-9b71-e3d7fdfbde49" :question/math-academy-id "q-364639" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5527a2df-a2d6-5eff-8e35-66b2ae6e3318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5527a2df-a2d6-5eff-8e35-66b2ae6e3318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321819} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdcaa383-804e-5471-975f-ae53c9f7d704" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e9ff4e40-981d-5344-8025-a467141eea82" :question/math-academy-id "q-364640" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "115a27e6-69f7-5ae7-ae89-7370d3de0757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321283} {:answer/id #uuid "1919937a-2afb-50ef-a435-2e2586b2ccf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321345} {:answer/id #uuid "1fc3e1db-f5f9-598a-b7f8-db2c3fd3fd46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321385} {:answer/id #uuid "23de5c13-9552-53a1-b324-42e2d6bbb04e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321423} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd4fe2b-59cd-55ee-9d03-6090d8c7e680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1919937a-2afb-50ef-a435-2e2586b2ccf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321345} :answer-field/id #uuid "abbe930b-f8f7-558e-aba8-3023dce76a4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08e9021a-643b-5729-9c28-b1707d9de34c" :question/math-academy-id "q-365299" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}\\le 3\\sqrt{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x} & \\le 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x} & \\le 3\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x} & \\le 9\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e8b3b6f-beec-552d-9868-ed6a0f8961df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321259} {:answer/id #uuid "1c557786-2967-59f8-9bd5-79da655905ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321362} {:answer/id #uuid "5f13a860-aaa5-50a7-bd99-bf37fedc3d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321896} {:answer/id #uuid "7aeb5e32-2058-558c-8345-f421ebabdca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322111} {:answer/id #uuid "cd89b622-3090-56f6-b614-bf5d1b191223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c557786-2967-59f8-9bd5-79da655905ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321362} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec706b48-8cdc-560f-b7d1-8876a9a34e0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4215e98b-bff5-5507-9fa2-a2d9f87a2aa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-365326" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2}>{3}^{2-3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2} & >{3}^{2-3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡[3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2}] & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2-3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2}] & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2-3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}) & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(x-2)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(x-2)(0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(2-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb9a735-2591-53ae-942e-27326f05c5a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321365} {:answer/id #uuid "855765a6-c18c-5fd7-aff3-ae9f740b5f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322201} {:answer/id #uuid "9b4a67bf-2910-5ff0-8c64-ff503d74b674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322377} {:answer/id #uuid "9f100973-b174-52ef-a6df-75a6b6c21f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "855765a6-c18c-5fd7-aff3-ae9f740b5f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322201} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a21df8-6735-53f7-ab0d-60e13e0d8a56" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323230} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ca56064-d1a3-5c0b-9fb2-4735a5356b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(2-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321492} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6fc383-06f8-50a9-8448-ef484256d26a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322133} {:answer/id #uuid "d300781a-7ab5-51eb-9dc7-ddbdf2036e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322797} {:answer/id #uuid "e6cd5d3b-afe9-59e8-9ebe-5df84d2f261d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t(1-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d6fc383-06f8-50a9-8448-ef484256d26a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322133} :answer-field/id #uuid "84e7b7a9-a761-5a17-a1f6-84fc6f78eb63" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df1e3a35-d035-58fe-be59-0f6465258bfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-365332" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt+1)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+1} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt+1}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt+1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt+1 \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cb6f943-3e8e-524b-bbee-63f9e2fdf804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321754} {:answer/id #uuid "54d366a4-512b-5fdd-9452-97813292a5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321814} {:answer/id #uuid "6f858266-867e-57c2-9517-65c1befc7cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322012} {:answer/id #uuid "cd53bee2-25b5-5e3c-aed5-988b8fa7045b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd53bee2-25b5-5e3c-aed5-988b8fa7045b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322745} :answer-field/id #uuid "04ca1972-a54a-5852-a21a-a87c720ccc46" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323174} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4c9f40-c6e2-5853-8638-356f8cad7b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321258} {:answer/id #uuid "32813799-ef84-58e9-be1c-355927fc7c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{t(3-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321532} {:answer/id #uuid "4c24bdc6-7b19-5dd6-a83b-07278fa751a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{t(1-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321745} {:answer/id #uuid "cbefe572-e72b-538f-9b17-156c67a987e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c24bdc6-7b19-5dd6-a83b-07278fa751a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{t(1-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321745} :answer-field/id #uuid "efeea11b-c191-58de-8be1-d05cf1c4bccf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00d0a81e-18e6-583c-927a-87161040247c" :question/math-academy-id "q-365372" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+3t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}+3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+3\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+3\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt+3)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt+3}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt+3}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt+3 \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{3}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{3}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{3}{t(1-t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{3}{t(1-t)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b6ef500-69ff-5a40-b48f-e088f52d064e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321609} {:answer/id #uuid "41c0fa4d-a126-55ae-9c76-9a51eaa582da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321668} {:answer/id #uuid "62b0acb1-9d3b-5014-b1bf-5710aa542d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321916} {:answer/id #uuid "b45af41b-34de-5ff1-9276-8aba652c1ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b45af41b-34de-5ff1-9276-8aba652c1ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322556} :answer-field/id #uuid "76f2eb47-f60c-52cf-b160-334d25e2b44c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323400} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e30663a-b390-5830-9a84-dc8051984585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321891} {:answer/id #uuid "863d1538-1168-517a-85c4-b6d2f6ebd3e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322213} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6efe1b-c862-552d-9ced-a139644857f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322405} {:answer/id #uuid "fb7b0d79-f075-5a45-a49c-489071c257d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-1}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "863d1538-1168-517a-85c4-b6d2f6ebd3e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322213} :answer-field/id #uuid "97e590c8-328b-57ee-86d8-da7fa00acd41" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c23b4e5b-85ca-5730-b4e3-0f8ebb92441d" :question/math-academy-id "q-365373" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}-t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt-1)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt-1} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt-1}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt-1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt-1 \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{-1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{-1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33ce3693-01de-5937-98a5-950f5433a6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321540} {:answer/id #uuid "65e5517e-cf2c-5731-a1c0-3c543be6b09d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321936} {:answer/id #uuid "9991e042-4e75-5222-93c4-5563e233ad9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322367} {:answer/id #uuid "fe04bc55-e4b6-5550-9da1-eee961886cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9991e042-4e75-5222-93c4-5563e233ad9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322367} :answer-field/id #uuid "01d07bac-0abb-5ec1-be1f-93cfaff8da9c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323168} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0392f3cb-4495-5d45-8af7-ce324dce5c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321180} {:answer/id #uuid "8ecc4064-5657-5ce5-b134-f78f11f3c2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322281} {:answer/id #uuid "b980b6a7-aeff-58c4-9680-5fe1df4c9844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322586} {:answer/id #uuid "be505855-bad5-5c93-997b-278b7d7030eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be505855-bad5-5c93-997b-278b7d7030eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322625} :answer-field/id #uuid "8142cc63-340f-5ac3-8562-6ecae5fa9382" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4790775-7e4a-5504-8e0d-e0c3f96089b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-365376" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=xsin\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=xsin\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)} \\\\ (t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1 \\\\ (t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)} & =\\frac{1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t} & =\\frac{1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083ce095-68ad-5438-94f0-d1ad6f101d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321211} {:answer/id #uuid "34fe18e9-f3bb-5fa3-acad-95f48e7d367d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321554} {:answer/id #uuid "409b6d70-1792-5fef-b190-3dd3abe8fb25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{1-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321659} {:answer/id #uuid "feaadcbe-e1b9-52dd-8dd7-fc16d79bdfa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34fe18e9-f3bb-5fa3-acad-95f48e7d367d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321554} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6c4774f-3c94-544e-908b-0eb14e4f508a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323527} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a42e7b3-d323-5e4f-aab0-f221c70f3d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{1-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321227} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce9ae65-0f3d-54c1-be6e-2b05b2eca587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2t}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321496} {:answer/id #uuid "326eab8c-35e4-5977-b209-f7e40e3283aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{1-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321530} {:answer/id #uuid "9363d3d5-2e3e-5993-8d77-34d875a2ec62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9363d3d5-2e3e-5993-8d77-34d875a2ec62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322315} :answer-field/id #uuid "c66d067e-ab94-5526-aae7-9dc2d887352b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7908dcc8-2ade-5e7c-a576-33b72695a96c" :question/math-academy-id "q-365377" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}-{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2}) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2}) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-2t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-2t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt-2t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt-2t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt-2t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt-2t}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt-2t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt-2t \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =-2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{-2t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{-2}{1-t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{2}{t-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1356f59e-04a0-51af-b39a-7a860708c3ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321298} {:answer/id #uuid "4815746e-9fe7-539d-bb32-793af7e9957f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321713} {:answer/id #uuid "66871d6a-5f30-57dc-aab8-2f465b099010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321946} {:answer/id #uuid "b6be468e-9df3-54ae-95ee-720737fa9931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322569} {:answer/id #uuid "de477895-a349-5571-bcb4-68d3a6dae781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-3}{t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6be468e-9df3-54ae-95ee-720737fa9931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322569} :answer-field/id #uuid "46bfce3a-67d1-5c28-b61a-50c95e8fe3cc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323301} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "715eabdc-86cf-5d93-9a01-c244be23d0c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(t)}{t-\\operatorname{cos}(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322035} {:answer/id #uuid "8978f549-c0cc-5f49-b167-b1bbfd0d1d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322243} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f5ea56-0bf4-536f-8f77-7a8a78b9ab0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322554} {:answer/id #uuid "b651a5fc-749c-5e54-bee1-770a1f257175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322565} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9ec20f-8666-5e66-83d9-781f37295c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "715eabdc-86cf-5d93-9a01-c244be23d0c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(t)}{t-\\operatorname{cos}(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322035} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cdc0e39-ed32-5eeb-926d-95337276b33a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "770dbe98-faf2-5844-9ee4-25631476a1a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-365378" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=xcos\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=xcos\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-3t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3t) \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3t) \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-3\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-3\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =-(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ -\\frac{t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t} \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t} & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b0add5f-c19d-5f7d-b4b9-e7062ba28afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321236} {:answer/id #uuid "1383eca2-1d3c-5452-9f16-5b57846c8ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321302} {:answer/id #uuid "328f935e-4f79-575c-8650-32246bcbde6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(2-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321533} {:answer/id #uuid "bbdff64e-9e6d-5d36-a63a-0886d3593aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-t)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b0add5f-c19d-5f7d-b4b9-e7062ba28afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321236} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d7ecd92-2965-5e0b-8ff2-d5187ef57cc2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323265} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16113a68-63f6-51b7-80c7-694e17ec8275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321326} {:answer/id #uuid "761a80b9-a338-5077-b1a9-34500f7337d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322068} {:answer/id #uuid "c087c2ce-d887-5dc3-9fbc-556f58ff9028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322643} {:answer/id #uuid "e015adb9-200c-5008-8368-227bbc42ec0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c087c2ce-d887-5dc3-9fbc-556f58ff9028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322643} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea6c9ca6-8473-52aa-9173-527222805616" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ce629b8-462b-5d82-9660-148c84cf68f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-365383" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+ln\\,(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+ln\\,(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{1+t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{1+t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t})\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t}}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t}}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =\\frac{1}{1+t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{1+t}}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-t)(1+t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323819 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11628838-ceb0-5b74-98b6-2dd123af86b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321284} {:answer/id #uuid "964e9dbc-bbfe-59f0-8200-6778e46d6fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322344} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2a4b52-d4d4-5c3d-9b8d-ebaeed6556cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{1-{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322979} {:answer/id #uuid "efdb785f-6b02-5a9c-9163-f62b35c1c8d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1-{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "964e9dbc-bbfe-59f0-8200-6778e46d6fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322344} :answer-field/id #uuid "01528867-afe9-5506-a703-235285b924b1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08890123-cb85-52f4-b0be-9b16bcda356f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t}{1-{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321213} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd07b27-d464-5584-b6d0-d002eb20af4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322874} {:answer/id #uuid "efdef6f2-0e4c-5436-b3aa-f2f67ad7e71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323038} {:answer/id #uuid "f762776a-c252-56bd-ae32-b06a092e03d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcd07b27-d464-5584-b6d0-d002eb20af4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322874} :answer-field/id #uuid "662ab64b-3ea7-51f2-9233-f643b9f0d225" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa5839f3-ad78-59e3-af24-cb3ab8949d40" :question/math-academy-id "q-365386" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{3}+ln\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{3}+ln\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{3}+3x{t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{3} & =3x{t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-{t}^{2}) & =(\\frac{3x{t}^{3}+1}{t})\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}{3x{t}^{3}+1} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}{3x{t}^{3}+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3x{t}^{3}+1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3x{t}^{3}+1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})} \\\\ {t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3x{t}^{3}+1 \\\\ {t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-3x{t}^{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{3x{t}^{3}}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})} & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{3tx}{1-{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{3tx}{{t}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{3t}{{t}^{2}-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323820 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ab4742-91b1-56b7-aeb7-0cb999a0542e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14t}{t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321431} {:answer/id #uuid "55455322-1286-534f-9707-b6847e5a22a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321821} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea48740-8e2d-55a4-8716-3d4ab1ffab89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14t}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322144} {:answer/id #uuid "839a652d-0fac-544b-99a3-1dd16b8d6f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322185} {:answer/id #uuid "f13a54a9-d395-5742-82d1-cd829d5a84de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14t}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ea48740-8e2d-55a4-8716-3d4ab1ffab89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14t}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322144} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfb28582-831f-5528-aa50-93a3ad1c3ead" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323575} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20aa476b-da98-524e-b2e5-741bcbef96ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321394} {:answer/id #uuid "21d9386f-c921-55ca-9dba-d8569e22ff55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321402} {:answer/id #uuid "220c2dd2-3764-5d1d-94c2-e69733949f3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321405} {:answer/id #uuid "5b42c510-520d-5d10-b4c6-947a90b924b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321867} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d67b64-3484-53f4-bf25-b7a1f92e7c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{14t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20aa476b-da98-524e-b2e5-741bcbef96ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321394} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee8458e3-4613-5e41-8e43-3ef9d7587b58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f9eb0d3-58fa-594c-8c10-ad8b6522e3fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-365388" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=3xcos\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+7{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=3xcos\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+7{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+7{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+7{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{t}^{2}) \\\\ t & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+3x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{t}^{2}) \\\\ t & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+14t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+14t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =(14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} \\\\ (t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =14t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =\\frac{14t}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{14t}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ba2082f-2026-51ae-946b-513ddff3f786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{\\pi }^{3}-6{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321483} {:answer/id #uuid "a55f1bbb-b59a-58f0-989d-7368f34c43bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322444} {:answer/id #uuid "d0aa616c-04f0-5a28-9051-a69a89515d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi -6{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322777} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b49814-bd66-5fc4-af87-a060b14a242d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\pi }^{3}-6{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322832} {:answer/id #uuid "dda5ae9d-e68f-5bfa-a58b-bf49578992ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\pi +6{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a55f1bbb-b59a-58f0-989d-7368f34c43bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322444} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa8778a8-2084-5de3-8964-7ea39574c75a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3a7d984f-6568-5953-af07-5e17745cd0e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-36559" :question/problem "Suppose that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂z}=3{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+3x{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\n$$\n\nEvaluate the following expression at the point $(\\pi ,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂y}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂z∂z}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂y∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+3x{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =6y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+6xyz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)-3{x}^{3}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂z∂z} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+3x{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)+3x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+6{x}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(\\pi ,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂y}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1,1)} & =6(-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+6(\\pi )(-1)(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-3(\\pi )^{3}(1)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )=6\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂z∂z}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1,1)} & =3(\\pi )(-1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3(\\pi )(-1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-3(\\pi )^{2}(-1)^{2}(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+6(\\pi )^{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )=-6\\pi -6{\\pi }^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂y}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1,1)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂z∂z}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1,1)} & =6\\pi +(-6\\pi -6{\\pi }^{2})=-6{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b14a2ba-037b-5808-ab5c-1471b3b9efcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322113} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6055d0-fc1a-5cd1-b93a-ac0ad546d0ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322132} {:answer/id #uuid "8150aa80-6174-5846-8338-8a2330df4293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322164} {:answer/id #uuid "b39390ee-a86a-537c-be7c-ac51d056917c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322550} {:answer/id #uuid "e83c21db-4970-5c82-a74c-5bd63c4039dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b14a2ba-037b-5808-ab5c-1471b3b9efcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322113} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f5fd4f2-8722-5896-8d2f-2c556f5eb935" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4788079-739f-5332-8879-eb48a6a8359b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36752" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 10 & -8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 10 & -8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 10 & -8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -8 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{5}{4}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{8}(7{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(7(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1423f464-beef-5e99-9409-b3d4ac5043ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321310} {:answer/id #uuid "7b755dec-3573-55e0-b187-1c70939adcdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322120} {:answer/id #uuid "806e3e30-0291-58e6-abb6-ecce94953100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322156} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce25d70-41a1-50f0-91bf-535055888ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322384} {:answer/id #uuid "b718991a-198e-56d5-a300-ebd932e0ab6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b755dec-3573-55e0-b187-1c70939adcdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322120} :answer-field/id #uuid "aea0db33-c7cf-5915-89f1-c5178160661f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65ec0581-72cb-57ee-83e7-13061ed24107" :question/math-academy-id "q-36947" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -6 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -6 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -9 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -9 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-9){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -27 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=(-\\frac{1}{9}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{3}.$ From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{-3}{1}=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1369c0ac-54a3-5d47-a55e-32690a098f88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321299} {:answer/id #uuid "25431ce5-f607-5144-90be-7ae6626ad54c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321436} {:answer/id #uuid "42971dbc-8e46-565e-84b2-72ac7f895971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321674} {:answer/id #uuid "53cc0f39-7dba-5571-9d2a-0a77806f1ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321807} {:answer/id #uuid "85baf665-9e38-55d9-9223-b973f70f3e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25431ce5-f607-5144-90be-7ae6626ad54c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321436} :answer-field/id #uuid "7887e763-fbe8-534a-becc-d42df52a2ad2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82d92314-f305-5f0c-b4d3-f0a08c54fb3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37290" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /3)}({tan}^{2}⁡(x)+3{cot}^{2}⁡(x)).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /3)}({tan}^{2}⁡(x)+3{cot}^{2}⁡(x)) & ={tan}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3{cot}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))}^{2}+3{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =3+3(\\frac{3}{9}) \\\\  & =3+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a29aa16-6c63-5a61-91a9-1f74b2ed3f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321225} {:answer/id #uuid "139de0b1-a7cc-5a6f-8d11-174a489bfe2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321305} {:answer/id #uuid "3a995098-985b-5a0d-8363-a15a9776bdf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321596} {:answer/id #uuid "76e233f9-7d82-54a2-b6d4-56c363f79142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322074} {:answer/id #uuid "a544f028-4f1a-5cd3-8842-d28c764161a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a995098-985b-5a0d-8363-a15a9776bdf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321596} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ac795a-9483-57f9-a1e0-931dea19d10f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f559f7f4-e02a-5090-9ef8-87c06ed535b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-37399" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}21 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -12 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}21 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -12 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -7\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}20{x}_{1}-13{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}-13{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 18{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{9}{10}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10} & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{20}(13{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(13(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+9{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26853257-a15d-56bd-baf3-05cb4af2b356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321448} {:answer/id #uuid "6215dc3a-949b-5a88-b400-1754de3451a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321914} {:answer/id #uuid "8f37a9ee-9183-5f94-b9e6-589f18d0723c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322284} {:answer/id #uuid "97007dd8-0a0c-5e5d-b2fe-2c703399817a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322352} {:answer/id #uuid "e831241f-8157-56bb-8b58-0b3139bec9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26853257-a15d-56bd-baf3-05cb4af2b356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321448} :answer-field/id #uuid "001c22af-200a-5e74-a872-4fc6a4c58849" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b75fe299-3a00-56ce-ac2b-3a6b8472e075" :question/math-academy-id "q-37401" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(3)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{12}{5}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5988b748-db6c-5b36-9840-e6478cabbb51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321853} {:answer/id #uuid "7c472c58-699c-5782-89ce-b06e067314de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322125} {:answer/id #uuid "cf3e7fab-ed01-5f57-aecb-974f3540bbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322766} {:answer/id #uuid "d0690c0d-0bea-5716-9be4-2e7a0d6ed043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322774} {:answer/id #uuid "dc30c442-053c-588a-a4c2-b47c4d9a660e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf3e7fab-ed01-5f57-aecb-974f3540bbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322766} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb178049-21d1-5f35-85d8-2469af417f05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8f655022-c86d-5349-9391-8c2ff380546e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37403" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{20}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=4{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2(4{x}_{3})-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-4{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{-4}{1}=-4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d2a009-9a40-5d25-99e2-4f97af963231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321192} {:answer/id #uuid "2c533ee8-f9db-5f4a-a560-8035da471b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321490} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0552d2-cfa6-5c1e-901c-be3cad62fa64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321590} {:answer/id #uuid "70ec84ef-6655-53bf-9eb0-7897a3753db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322030} {:answer/id #uuid "80d50f5a-f000-539b-a1db-5c8a13952505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80d50f5a-f000-539b-a1db-5c8a13952505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322160} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b2db87a-5385-53da-8e27-89a6fe772ff2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39356ac1-0a31-5e6e-9a6f-6a96bbbbe55c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37683" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{x}^{1/x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}-x]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{1}{3}+1-(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{11}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21fa64d5-074d-5702-bcf4-064fabb99721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ x-2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,x,z\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454955} {:answer/id #uuid "4263713e-2f96-539b-8f35-ecc62bb28c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2x-3y\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,x,y\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455014} {:answer/id #uuid "4e5c75b7-6345-538e-96b8-2ba5d8c8cf18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,z\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455041} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac4954c-967a-55e2-9e2d-f8a985c1ff0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,z\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455063} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c02bca-de60-5fa3-8668-018b71b4b4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}y+z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,y,z\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e5c75b7-6345-538e-96b8-2ba5d8c8cf18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,z\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455041} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7e412b-267f-58a3-94eb-d3421d219bcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d0e70ad-1c81-5568-a326-e79afb6bb189" :question/math-academy-id "q-37827" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-9x-3y+3z \\\\ -12x-4y+4z \\\\ 6x+8y+10z\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSetting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-2z.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9x-3y+3z & =0 \\\\ -9x-3(-2z)+3z & =0 \\\\ -9x+9z & =0 \\\\ x & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,z\\in R\\}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b726bfa-ba4c-5d91-9f6d-6a3764d8755d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321242} {:answer/id #uuid "1594e17d-7011-5f7d-aa6a-12336ec25a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321322} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5f94c5-0d1b-5f79-944c-8cb9d821beed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321479} {:answer/id #uuid "564621d9-f224-5fff-b159-cf3615e51a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321828} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f698ad-bda0-5b89-8175-c64721d84bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b5f94c5-0d1b-5f79-944c-8cb9d821beed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321479} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb45d35-7041-5bf4-8524-44adec58ddbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad4a8e76-c297-5cff-8131-747df131b332" :question/math-academy-id "q-37865" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the line\n$y=1-x$ and the coordinate axes." :question/worked-solution "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/95/95ebd0a4eb874972b459e53eecf1d57650f63284698fcad967c09620ac01cd99.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=1-x$ intercepts the $x$-axis at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/d0/d048f20f2299722cee598cc1005237627ecdd7643e996b269f26b5a096f01f2f.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{-x+1}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{1}{1+x+y}]}_{0}^{-x+1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{1+x+(1-x)}-(-\\frac{1}{1+x+0}))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-(0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a4cafde-db4c-54bb-bab3-013f762c6d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321731} {:answer/id #uuid "4e99ea8e-9a16-57a0-baea-c6ae781ca29a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321765} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf875a9-930b-5577-b821-7ead99c40d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321884} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe73b65-ebc3-5cff-9a52-a69da7741fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322150} {:answer/id #uuid "b124652f-7ab9-5b4c-9413-d3dfdcb4c6a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e99ea8e-9a16-57a0-baea-c6ae781ca29a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321765} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce29bc07-9e48-58fd-9669-3eeb517f79e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "565688d3-a42b-5b17-9265-a372de4b8754" :question/math-academy-id "q-37899" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4fdf6f8d819a4ee043b1cc3b86d795e3166ebfe181e0958246dc084c2f9c6fc.png)\n\nThe point $P$ lies on the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\frac{3}{7},$ use the Pythagorean Identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cb/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{3}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{40}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8126b5-2054-5d88-865f-be6cbe674d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321368} {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} {:answer/id #uuid "51eee8c3-23ea-5613-a452-4c2182222cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321794} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba4b8d7-052a-5cf2-86db-50636fe22d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321871} {:answer/id #uuid "c2131ada-4ea2-5f29-85f1-aa42dc442a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} :answer-field/id #uuid "617b107f-dc16-5899-aab5-ab0f5850cc8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9112180a-ac6b-5472-ba8b-819d346ead1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37915" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c36dbce847c719accc77fa15f33dfb9a87cfae669691afe30f7693663076d2e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/52/52fe94bb303a001e89f9db221e9730d4ca8c5c284217aa20fb6084ee041dec66.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{10}{16}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{20}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "264c2a12-e0c5-5a72-8a56-3afd346a3797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321444} {:answer/id #uuid "27c94ab9-3f27-5b94-9153-03a51a8506f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321458} {:answer/id #uuid "28553fdc-9d2d-5736-815e-a08f21b3eb0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321462} {:answer/id #uuid "892d722c-b7e1-5e03-b74a-cd70d864499f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322240} {:answer/id #uuid "c7bbf3fb-8f2a-5c40-ba48-859a8ad13ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "264c2a12-e0c5-5a72-8a56-3afd346a3797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321444} :answer-field/id #uuid "6846a47f-81a7-5c49-bfa1-5b3dcabbb036" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a9ac202-6009-5fae-8bac-c5238fbadec1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37947" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x)=4,$ then what is $f(\\frac{1}{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(\\frac{1}{2})={lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2328240e-4257-5bd9-a135-e92fd6cfcac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321415} {:answer/id #uuid "4590da6a-1d19-5e9e-8708-8e979eef5674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321698} {:answer/id #uuid "76e89d5a-25ad-5e91-8f79-538c049f2947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322075} {:answer/id #uuid "8602bb92-aa80-513a-bc71-45eb070984a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322211} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8c7eeb-1587-561c-88b4-d2fdd44746eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc8c7eeb-1587-561c-88b4-d2fdd44746eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322870} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc079157-7349-5c1d-b71e-8d2f3ca017ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "756c4008-7e12-5f70-b039-f70f52bc5864" :question/math-academy-id "q-37951" :question/problem "Evaluate the repeated integral\n${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{\\pi }{3{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{\\pi }{3{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[-\\frac{\\pi }{3y}]}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3\\cdot (-1)}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{3\\cdot (-2)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "640f91af-9af3-58fc-8a63-9b8699619aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324801} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b6266a-0178-58c9-afc0-b11ab26e4082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325351} {:answer/id #uuid "d6bbf60c-21a8-5de6-bc41-6cabb8ede0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325839} {:answer/id #uuid "ede03381-3d73-5674-a3cc-1b91d88fe642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326063} {:answer/id #uuid "ef576b5f-b070-572f-99c9-6414e6f1049a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6bbf60c-21a8-5de6-bc41-6cabb8ede0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325839} :answer-field/id #uuid "40ee238c-35a5-5dad-bb56-ba165c7577fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12b9ca6b-0ddb-5ee4-aeb2-e1fcfdc8bcb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-3850" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x{e}^{-y}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{{e}^{y}} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n{e}^{y}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c8a1483-7aea-506a-a4fc-1ccec710489d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324212} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4ea4bf-f87b-5b48-92dc-27fd4fef8e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324350} {:answer/id #uuid "30f7adc2-38f4-5f80-b3df-1c30ce0e7a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324362} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe7a3e7-23fc-5974-bc1a-f0da89ca6543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324626} {:answer/id #uuid "5e65b2e2-013e-5ebf-89da-b39afccbf471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f4ea4bf-f87b-5b48-92dc-27fd4fef8e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324350} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ec30624-d599-5c74-9abb-9ad63322e7da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "592b6ac1-ddb2-56aa-ae83-1783ef1ea8c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-38629" :question/problem "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $w-x+y,$ where $w,x,y,z$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}w \\\\ x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}w-3x-2z \\\\ 2x-2w-4y \\\\ 3w-y-10x-7z \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -10 & -1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -10 & -1 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=-8.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28cc5ee2-6da9-52c1-a92b-8edb26634139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324300} {:answer/id #uuid "47e93b3f-33d7-5090-b1fc-02844e927bff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324553} {:answer/id #uuid "a44579f4-a9e4-53da-a506-9eefbb4bf2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325367} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8f6f17-d42e-553d-a74b-0e76268b5611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325601} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b95922-aad1-5b77-a0d0-e6a698ab713b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28cc5ee2-6da9-52c1-a92b-8edb26634139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324300} :answer-field/id #uuid "36035083-9336-58a8-9968-10256743cb12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "688a2834-9fcd-5275-97a0-97e7aaa1b79c" :question/math-academy-id "q-38704" :question/problem "Given that $\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-8A+6I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-8A+6I) & =O \\\\ A-8I+6{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 6{A}^{-1} & =8I-A \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{4}{3}I-\\frac{1}{6}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{4}{3},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{6}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{4}{3}+(-\\frac{1}{6})=\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4752b046-3b7e-5b8b-bc64-961b93ceeafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bb82d2af5bba5f24727e80c1e748c9a830d9ea89dde2f641f7cc3062006d94a0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324549} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd16a56-1a91-577c-937f-a6234f7de2bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6c/6c2409230292f147b3f0f87dfda1fbbf48ad3d7f718dbe315b55c5fde04d6bf4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325330} {:answer/id #uuid "c3efa590-2060-5310-8fcd-6ed4b394e490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/38/38be17e7358b863fd00aa77b27a5d13d35e95f9553337fc71b21b1b65c2e8e6c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325655} {:answer/id #uuid "d31e12d5-0281-5042-a3db-54c4f4cf1403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f1/f12ef2075acd21307696bc9c7920ab72730662d4b3d7ff090df52f81c33ffa19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325805} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ab9bda-564c-5a98-9a03-afbb6e1d45a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/40f4e01f3e6f6f52d781e784c8788c6688fe0ea02d5164d3d19f46b2758fc895.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4752b046-3b7e-5b8b-bc64-961b93ceeafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bb82d2af5bba5f24727e80c1e748c9a830d9ea89dde2f641f7cc3062006d94a0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324549} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a3f99be-3ea4-5937-ab62-cf6cec0982a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d7da895d-6757-5ad2-9849-d7013a6b1846" :question/math-academy-id "q-39247" :question/problem "![](images/88/88be779d85ba51746abba7a71cad58b6986fd72a14ba4fc09e8b015494f57c0e.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a rotation-scaling matrix. So, we need to find its scale factor and the angle of rotation.\n\nWe can find the scale factor using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{det(A)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent $A$ as a product of a rotation and a scaling matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ clockwise by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$  and scale the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/f5/f5f7e7fa8d149ae9b6f07af57cbc8fc103b2d7c306aec98dbb2119145aa276ca.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e9c9635-514f-5437-89a3-2311516df929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324616} {:answer/id #uuid "542f73e6-a0ee-5f63-a36b-e67fde3a6341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324669} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa4c2bd-e1e8-5ef1-b567-38d573db5658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324998} {:answer/id #uuid "98e23fb5-b473-5394-a5ba-4656c5c07d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325270} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5e0be4-5f46-5ddf-98a2-9f9d363b7d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f5e0be4-5f46-5ddf-98a2-9f9d363b7d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325323} :answer-field/id #uuid "526c7e14-1815-5fc1-a1ad-f65ad4c3be74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d6ca191-15db-5023-b8de-7e13937e634b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39422" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path  $r(t)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t\\,i+2j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t+2)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t+2)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2t\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t+2)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,i) \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,dt \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-2(\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}-1) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35f28ae9-ad9a-5c26-9668-970408c70e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $29$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324412} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5c3799-5e4f-5a34-b947-5cfae02078cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $30$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324622} {:answer/id #uuid "b4bed55f-f824-5e81-9b0b-d1dabfeb7799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $31$st term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325524} {:answer/id #uuid "ca97757a-df6b-59a0-b351-58dd173e396f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $28$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325727} {:answer/id #uuid "f1773241-3243-58c4-b192-f56ea9ba3f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $33$rd term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f5c3799-5e4f-5a34-b947-5cfae02078cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $30$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324622} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b37db49-95d4-5e02-bb33-93b46e7afabc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "149ce21a-affa-5de1-9e5b-30002aa3b866" :question/math-academy-id "q-39647" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,2$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n18,\\,12,\\,8,\\,\\frac{16}{3},\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=18$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{12}{18}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =18{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,2.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,2 \\\\ 18{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <0.000\\,2 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.000\\,2}{18} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}+1≈29.159\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $30$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24eebd01-d696-5583-866b-bc7ee0975ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $42$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324275} {:answer/id #uuid "5efa3e05-6fbd-561f-aad6-9039ff65a874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $39$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324765} {:answer/id #uuid "869369c2-c60a-5bfe-9e90-d7fc384dd4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $38$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325115} {:answer/id #uuid "e425578a-3944-5da8-bbf6-a67efada93ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $41$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325979} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c67aa3-aae6-50c4-9e30-cdb98ed82129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $40$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7c67aa3-aae6-50c4-9e30-cdb98ed82129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $40$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326012} :answer-field/id #uuid "1793d83a-a4d4-5328-b990-9f3e282f524a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5d1d92f-ad9b-5aee-bc72-594097ea04d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39648" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.001$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n64,\\,48,\\,36,\\,27,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=64$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{48}{64}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.001.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.001 \\\\ 64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <0.001 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.001}{64} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1≈39.386\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $40$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.001.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1671731d-3648-566b-a97e-0eab45a82192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $26$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324170} {:answer/id #uuid "adc9a1d2-018c-5b4d-b2a7-e71f4743b8a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $24$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325443} {:answer/id #uuid "b04449b3-181f-52ec-a4e6-f5294ab1676d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $25$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325472} {:answer/id #uuid "c162f7c3-59c9-59a9-8259-0baed70f1b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $28$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325634} {:answer/id #uuid "c7102623-fbe7-5669-bec9-23ec007db5ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $27$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1671731d-3648-566b-a97e-0eab45a82192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $26$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324170} :answer-field/id #uuid "969cd1e3-10ff-5e2e-aafc-fe8f7c6ec716" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9734fb4c-2cb9-5537-8453-0174b5edd25a" :question/math-academy-id "q-39656" :question/problem "The $2$nd term of a geometric sequence with a positive common ratio is $\\frac{3}{2}$, and the $4$th term is $\\frac{2}{3}.$ Given that all terms of the sequence are positive, what is the first term of this sequence to be less than\n$0.000\\,11?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${a}_{4}=\\frac{2}{3}$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{4-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{\\frac{2}{3}}{\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ r & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the common ratio is positive, we take the positive value. Now, since ${a}_{4}=\\frac{2}{3}$ and  $r=\\frac{2}{3}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-1} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3} \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3-n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,11.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3-n} & <0.000\\,11 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3-n} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11) \\\\ (3-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11) \\\\ 3-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}-3 \\\\ n & >3-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n3-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}≈25.480\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $26$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,11.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6925c39d-5eee-5066-9364-c93cc422e7eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324849} {:answer/id #uuid "6a03c0ec-3285-524b-81a9-a9f09d83cc60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\pm \\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324858} {:answer/id #uuid "73bb46d6-e3fb-5b2f-b9f8-4708a1be1de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\pm 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324932} {:answer/id #uuid "881030c3-4124-56a3-aec8-0572df8d4522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\pm \\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325129} {:answer/id #uuid "ff4872eb-6ebb-5292-b61a-e02e9bd0a1b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "881030c3-4124-56a3-aec8-0572df8d4522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\pm \\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325129} :answer-field/id #uuid "408df224-3d41-549d-a1c5-527797909301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "adf56ecc-9b6a-5dbf-a683-6641f23bbf0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-39862" :question/problem "Find $k$, if $u=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ \\frac{k}{9} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{8}{9}\\end{bmatrix}$ is a unit vector." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the norm of the vector $x$ in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u‖ & =\\sqrt{{u}_{1}^{2}+{u}_{2}^{2}+{u}_{3}^{2}+{u}_{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}+{(\\frac{k}{9})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{8}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{81}+\\frac{{k}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{64}{81}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{74+{k}^{2}}{81}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given equation  $‖u‖=1,$ we obtain an equation that we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{74+{k}^{2}}{81}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{74+{k}^{2}} & =9 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{74+{k}^{2}})}^{2} & ={9}^{2} \\\\ 74+{k}^{2} & =81 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =7 \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326788 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2935c965-3577-5dfe-ae8a-9b87acf0d993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{14.21}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324303} {:answer/id #uuid "477d3d8c-b227-51a2-94d4-fffe1c799e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{54.05}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324551} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9c4cd1-8775-55a9-8f5f-e12814da13fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45.12}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324862} {:answer/id #uuid "8beb8a2b-fdbb-55aa-95bc-2738740a0bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{34.05}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325161} {:answer/id #uuid "db5e5c22-3652-5b8c-b4b6-6526f0290ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{22.21}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db5e5c22-3652-5b8c-b4b6-6526f0290ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{22.21}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325883} :answer-field/id #uuid "83132e5b-ab8f-5767-b31f-36b261cf9c0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21e1141a-cb0f-578b-8a6b-1baafee0f9ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-39877" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$   \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{14}\\sqrt{12}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{2\\sqrt{42}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{42}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{42}})≈{22.21}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "502004df-a0be-5cea-878e-09803c5e34d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324628} {:answer/id #uuid "5babf9ef-2007-5ac3-b1a7-d3a7000142a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324728} {:answer/id #uuid "9f32f367-c166-57dd-a1ca-a5e258aafc79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325321} {:answer/id #uuid "cb26728e-8a5c-5d88-940f-50ca2ae95d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{x}+1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325734} {:answer/id #uuid "f04334c1-fe98-564e-8bda-076b98d63f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4{e}^{x}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "502004df-a0be-5cea-878e-09803c5e34d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324628} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fa3b410-49d2-532c-b067-882d1961f404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45459917-ee60-5543-8543-ded7c1f55344" :question/math-academy-id "q-4016" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x-2y}$ that satisfies $y(0)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x-2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{-2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2y}} \\\\ {e}^{2y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{2y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2y} & ={e}^{x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{2y} & =2{e}^{x}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{0}+C) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+C) & =0 \\\\ 2+C & =1 \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x-2y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=0$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1651ca76-dc02-593a-b74a-9ebdfdf7e0ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324167} {:answer/id #uuid "676512d3-d706-553c-bf00-3ae03b260a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324833} {:answer/id #uuid "80810967-fafe-59a7-9867-7ba0e17b74df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325046} {:answer/id #uuid "af353226-1810-59ec-9900-335d6c159018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,072" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325462} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e9ea61-3424-58e3-92ce-5f2d4b3ba4c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,048" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5e9ea61-3424-58e3-92ce-5f2d4b3ba4c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,048" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325833} :answer-field/id #uuid "66b72cda-b726-5b7e-9a11-045423483304" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c1a5b4e2-bd28-51a9-aba1-0a9bdcd42e00" :question/math-academy-id "q-40178" :question/problem "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a fourth term equal to $24$ and a seventh term equal to $\\frac{3}{8}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{4}=24$ and ${a}_{7}=\\frac{3}{8}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{7-4} & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{8})}{24} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =(\\frac{3}{8})(\\frac{1}{24}) \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nfor the $n$th term of the geometric sequence, we can find the first term. Indeed, for $n=4$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}(r)^{3} \\\\ 24 & ={a}_{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3} \\\\ 24 & ={a}_{1}(\\frac{1}{64}) \\\\ {a}_{1} & =1\\,536\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,536}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,536}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =1\\,536(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\,048.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1057d0ab-c433-53fc-84a5-9ffb065d34b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467464} {:answer/id #uuid "339ba22e-0df0-517c-95d1-7e6077efd09c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1057d0ab-c433-53fc-84a5-9ffb065d34b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467464} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c41882d-c836-503b-8dc9-ef3e36f9bdcf" :answer-field/key "$1+5+25+125+⋯$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468453} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c01b25f9-e4e2-5ecf-b963-555e12dbb905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467466} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc09b19-9928-52c0-8f2a-bf84b9daf6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c01b25f9-e4e2-5ecf-b963-555e12dbb905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467466} :answer-field/id #uuid "088b4927-cb51-5e0e-91b7-d85a0ed9f3b1" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{56}{9}+\\frac{784}{27}+⋯$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468454} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7456d2ff-df6b-523e-a46a-5cd5f5c8c23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467468} {:answer/id #uuid "1b01a857-dc53-56a9-808d-76ab0abefd7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b01a857-dc53-56a9-808d-76ab0abefd7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467469} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7c5c6d1-7639-509f-b20e-bfa9b6e5b570" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}0.1+0.01+0.001+0.000\\,1+⋯\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80009f32-0755-50c0-b5e3-ae14ffcb07ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-40282" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $1+5+25+125+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{56}{9}+\\frac{784}{27}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{4}{3})}{(\\frac{2}{7})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $\\begin{gathered}0.1+0.01+0.001+0.000\\,1+⋯\\end{gathered}$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{0.01}{0.1} \\\\  & =0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series $\\begin{gathered}0.1+0.01+0.001+0.000\\,1+⋯\\end{gathered}$ converges."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3338f747-4407-580b-8373-034eea69c03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{512}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324381} {:answer/id #uuid "52f3eb31-63e7-538f-a22f-a60a27811dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324652} {:answer/id #uuid "5c863b96-f89c-50b4-9708-f1c41dd56fd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{64}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324739} {:answer/id #uuid "65e45a81-823c-51df-998b-b2cd6f5b105f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324816} {:answer/id #uuid "7249a2a6-6174-56da-91ff-8ad56fef9814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{224}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3338f747-4407-580b-8373-034eea69c03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{512}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324381} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9dff424-5bd2-5785-a6d9-b72701469d49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a5e7defe-c829-569a-9771-9c0e8f2ed047" :question/math-academy-id "q-40310" :question/problem "Consider the negative infinite geometric sequence that has a third term equal to $-1$ and a seventh term equal to $-\\frac{1}{4\\,096}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that $4\\,096={8}^{4}.$*" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{3}=-1$ and ${a}_{7}=-\\frac{1}{4\\,096}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{7-3} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{4\\,096})}{(-1)} \\\\ {r}^{4} & =\\frac{1}{4\\,096} \\\\ {r}^{4} & ={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{4} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[4]{{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{4}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have taken the positive fourth root since we're told that the sequence is negative.\n\nNow, using the formula\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nfor the $n$th term of the geometric sequence, we can find the first term. Indeed, for $n=3$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{2} \\\\ -1 & ={a}_{1}{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2} \\\\ -1 & ={a}_{1}(\\frac{1}{64}) \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-64}{(1-\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{64}{(\\frac{7}{8})} \\\\  & =-64\\cdot \\frac{8}{7} \\\\  & =-\\frac{512}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f0a06d8-2d1a-56c8-be6c-0de612626107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324346} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6d60ca-7ba6-582b-8ba8-aa6a2bb6f45a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324870} {:answer/id #uuid "86278f77-2ac1-53b5-bab8-a9ea417f8fd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325108} {:answer/id #uuid "d75de039-1846-5a30-9cbb-9b88d55e855c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325843} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d2beef-eaea-5c96-adf4-c346ba078cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c6d60ca-7ba6-582b-8ba8-aa6a2bb6f45a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324870} :answer-field/id #uuid "108e1ca7-51ee-54a2-986d-908f36b9ba57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f73dfde-d126-5b7a-9d31-4cf2267cf6ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-40399" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{6}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -\\frac{37}{6} & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{37}{6}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{37}{6}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=6$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -37 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -37 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{1}{37}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fba6985-3012-59f9-9046-f7eb81bd2134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467470} {:answer/id #uuid "4176abbf-bd07-546f-8c5b-1cbab5b0a94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4176abbf-bd07-546f-8c5b-1cbab5b0a94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467471} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3b98d53-5717-5f50-8dd4-dd0c7af69429" :answer-field/key "The number $-3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468456} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88ac396c-2449-52a9-a0d6-33a2b4292428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467472} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ec575c-69f5-5fb8-9972-08b07552919e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8ec575c-69f5-5fb8-9972-08b07552919e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467473} :answer-field/id #uuid "95a51358-016b-50f8-9abd-1bdef89debaa" :answer-field/key "The number $2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468457} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa42429c-3d19-5a80-9479-8973b3e68f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467474} {:answer/id #uuid "5098c1b0-3b23-5a63-8473-81c712f3c23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa42429c-3d19-5a80-9479-8973b3e68f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467474} :answer-field/id #uuid "694365a2-c4b5-5df8-91a8-0c68236bf025" :answer-field/key "The number $10$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0164c43-f6ad-58eb-9330-df1855ede5c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-40556" :question/problem "Which of the following values ​are possible outputs of $f(x)=-5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x-1}+3?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The range of ${(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}>0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x-1}>0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-5$ and then adding $3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x-1} & >0 \\\\ -5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x-1} & <0 \\\\ -5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x-1}+3 & <0+3 \\\\ f(x) & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)<3.$ Thus, of the given values, only $y=-3$ and $y=2$ are  outputs of $y=f(x).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b220b9c53bfaa49fe9b1b1c70fb9e21d08e247f44df8716846d08fd37ac2cf11.png)\n\n  \n\nSo, the correct answer is “The number $-3$”; “The number $2$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ba46e1-9fb1-54f5-9f26-4a03d84e709f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{33.87}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323998} {:answer/id #uuid "2674114e-3fc0-5703-a213-4179de80a6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60.67}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324282} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb90082-fba8-5dc3-ba8e-438ce3fe2cf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{68.12}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324760} {:answer/id #uuid "883066a8-775d-587c-8c24-18ffbf7670b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60.01}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325130} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc50684-1de5-58d8-b274-f925b67abaef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{15.25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eb90082-fba8-5dc3-ba8e-438ce3fe2cf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{68.12}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324760} :answer-field/id #uuid "d39f6345-f523-582a-8ed1-bd373584a33a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d402119-a262-5f96-8245-1e50922a8694" :question/math-academy-id "q-40561" :question/problem "Rounded to two decimal places, what is the acute angle between the vectors  $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)(-1)+(2)(-1)+(3)(2)+(-4)(0)}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{6})≈{68.12}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05aa2b74-3473-50db-a3d9-fefeb5b23a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{87.24}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324023} {:answer/id #uuid "816f4158-b74f-583e-9a6d-e44c27a2e626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85.35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325054} {:answer/id #uuid "82d52b5b-2b69-5058-a805-f742853d81d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{83.24}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325072} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b306f2-05b0-566f-b4bb-3365478423d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{87.27}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325360} {:answer/id #uuid "a719e046-f4eb-571e-9d73-55b635635972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{84.32}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3b306f2-05b0-566f-b4bb-3365478423d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{87.27}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325360} :answer-field/id #uuid "114ad25d-2d18-5a8b-98d7-ea7680bff2b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "473a85e8-59ee-5be1-849d-d7a1fd3382ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-40562" :question/problem "Rounded to two decimal places, what is the acute angle between the vectors $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{8} \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)(4)+(\\sqrt{8})(1)+(-2)(\\sqrt{2})+(-3)(1)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}}\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{22}\\sqrt{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{110}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{110}}{220}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{110}}{220})≈{87.27}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326851 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2034a35c-608a-5377-bcc9-48a7416a53fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{62.39}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324238} {:answer/id #uuid "39783c3c-b1fa-53fa-a50f-e62d3a1a4fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{76.11}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324437} {:answer/id #uuid "8a311c9a-8ddb-5694-9ead-1d6ad34e14dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75.28}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325146} {:answer/id #uuid "92646c9e-9b3b-50a5-bd11-fd05c5b98607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{65.41}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325214} {:answer/id #uuid "c93e2f77-e7d5-503a-80e3-66969bfe4b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{82.33}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39783c3c-b1fa-53fa-a50f-e62d3a1a4fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{76.11}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324437} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b57e4ce-f680-5938-b7fe-4b8c76b3bfed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2b1b609-d6c1-5722-9004-d1cf10e1f7ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-40564" :question/problem "Rounded to two decimal places, what is the acute angle between the vectors  $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\text{ }-2\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula  \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-4)+(-2)\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{25}\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{6}{25})≈{76.11}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b702a71-f7bf-5ba7-a0e7-e0fa88d61a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324203} {:answer/id #uuid "487f1725-d816-5348-931c-2601e5df88f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324561} {:answer/id #uuid "71b95cf7-c7bd-5447-95ee-d498bf89a3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324910} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f09726-77e1-5376-989d-c9459e17a7d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325407} {:answer/id #uuid "b8de00ed-99d2-53f7-84ce-5bba33a863de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "487f1725-d816-5348-931c-2601e5df88f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324561} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8804d4c-29db-5710-97fe-6ec95143d3a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "695bf8dc-adcc-5ba6-b927-749766914d40" :question/math-academy-id "q-40565" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vectors\n $v=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7} \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7} \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ \\text{ }4\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}\\cdot (-\\sqrt{7})+7\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 4}{\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}+{7}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{7})^{2}+{3}^{2}+{7}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{64}\\sqrt{81}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{72} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d88320-9c11-5280-b76f-40d6819c8636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{82.34}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324026} {:answer/id #uuid "283eced6-cba8-5ab9-873f-cef502a919af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{70.76}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324294} {:answer/id #uuid "6620172a-f34f-54bf-b674-5f04d8ca274e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324821} {:answer/id #uuid "78b6dccb-d580-5fdc-8de6-f8a96aed4746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{72.25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324975} {:answer/id #uuid "9db5ec7a-7db2-58bf-963a-dfc9df68138a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{89.76}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "283eced6-cba8-5ab9-873f-cef502a919af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{70.76}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324294} :answer-field/id #uuid "68ef84f4-2d5d-528b-a1d0-77a23ef63f01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca41f5b8-b3cf-55a5-b73c-3a463b4f4955" :question/math-academy-id "q-40566" :question/problem "Rounded to two decimal places, what is the acute angle between the vectors  $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\text{ }1\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-5)+(-4)\\cdot (-5)+5\\cdot 2+(-5)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{5}^{2}+(-5)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{\\sqrt{67}\\sqrt{55}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{\\sqrt{3685}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{20}{\\sqrt{3685}})≈{70.76}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79387d8f-aec7-5a92-a157-3f5f266b213f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324980} {:answer/id #uuid "7acae3c0-88f3-5521-a92a-0c1ef5e48a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Inconclusive because $|r|=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325001} {:answer/id #uuid "987f66bd-4a09-54d5-b4e4-263358395517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325267} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a54f62-35f0-5596-ab48-8afd06881664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|<1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325533} {:answer/id #uuid "d4228613-730c-5fb1-8707-1a9fe9b9da56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "987f66bd-4a09-54d5-b4e4-263358395517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325267} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4ec3e5-461c-5932-91a5-7db234a8c289" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f208d59b-bccf-5fd6-a6b7-a683ef46db60" :question/math-academy-id "q-40577" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  ${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{15}{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{n-1}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=3.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{2}{15}{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{4-1}}{\\frac{2}{15}{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{3-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{3}}{{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "071c7ffc-f85c-55cb-bad7-47d48aa7c478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324036} {:answer/id #uuid "21d80a74-8ecf-57a0-acb2-52ebdc6fafa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324251} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3a375b-bf05-58be-9d97-905ba1068053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324878} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f97680-6e2c-590b-939e-f88d3b727056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325396} {:answer/id #uuid "f6932377-bb86-5697-a000-9089e702b5a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e3a375b-bf05-58be-9d97-905ba1068053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324878} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7ad1805-eaf0-5168-911d-4ae0db5ffd0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "342a1074-4db1-5f41-b2c0-7147d022edf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-40590" :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn+1}}{{4}^{n-1}}$ converge?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn+1}}{{4}^{n-1}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn}\\cdot 16}{{4}^{n}\\cdot {4}^{-1}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(16\\cdot 4){(\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }64{(\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{16}^{k}}{4} & <1 \\\\ {16}^{k} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${16}^{k}=4$ has the solution $k=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Now, since ${16}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${16}^{k}<4$ must be $k<\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23a80ebd-83ac-57fe-8d29-cf9d378a55e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324264} {:answer/id #uuid "8e054025-c750-507e-b378-275bfa2cff6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325176} {:answer/id #uuid "c69c4434-a2aa-5d28-8313-b21b21c08cbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325687} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6d1a6a-9da2-5d83-aa2c-926093703b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325754} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f6656e-2e27-5295-8486-ecf5d17ab06b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd6d1a6a-9da2-5d83-aa2c-926093703b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325754} :answer-field/id #uuid "e806f62c-89ec-51c4-a668-7516a58964fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c22b52e6-cf75-58b2-823a-b76f6234d620" :question/math-academy-id "q-40610" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 4}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-\\frac{2}{3})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =(-\\frac{2}{3})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{4}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x$ coincides with the projection, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is zero $0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10cc83e4-4a68-52d4-b338-032fbdc0c4f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324125} {:answer/id #uuid "3f470bd7-a807-5528-acc5-2a476fd2064d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324480} {:answer/id #uuid "712b96f9-5e05-5d1a-bb65-c463f83dfa57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324904} {:answer/id #uuid "af04c33b-d58b-555f-af8d-a4f9508a0b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325459} {:answer/id #uuid "bf00d910-6f67-537f-8bac-b8822a589211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf00d910-6f67-537f-8bac-b8822a589211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325614} :answer-field/id #uuid "180276bb-da02-5515-9e1f-76b1748b74f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8c7811a-eb0e-522e-b483-fedac22b4a18" :question/math-academy-id "q-40742" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 4}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{4}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=\\frac{10}{3},$ $c=\\frac{5}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{(\\frac{10}{3})}{(\\frac{5}{3})}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326877 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "921d74fc-5992-541a-8d34-262da2a022b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\,k,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325210} {:answer/id #uuid "98111b0f-d938-55b9-9d8e-57268bb41e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325262} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f999cc-4352-5167-b816-6505d62fe458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{k},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325568} {:answer/id #uuid "f55f820c-b442-563c-a6b3-2bb2362d37de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{k},-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326129} {:answer/id #uuid "f8156262-3b55-5a6e-8633-35834ee14eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\,k,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "921d74fc-5992-541a-8d34-262da2a022b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\,k,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325210} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ac33635-09ab-5b96-83ae-a3d4fd6f5795" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "74d79016-8845-5f4d-914d-8daddb3b0e8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-40761" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-k,k),$ where $k$ is a positive real number. What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-k)^{2}+{k}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{k}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-k}{k})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi $ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(\\sqrt{2}\\,k,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e428d06-0992-53e3-b2fa-04cd82f4f790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5k}{4},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324614} {:answer/id #uuid "6a1998ef-38cc-5359-9d79-21e98f166b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\,k}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324859} {:answer/id #uuid "922554be-9e96-502e-9651-e17b7937529e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\,k}{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325211} {:answer/id #uuid "941d3f7d-97aa-5af2-87df-3fc445a60c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\,k}{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325232} {:answer/id #uuid "cbdabdbf-600d-575d-9d92-a3eabdd12cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{k}{4},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "941d3f7d-97aa-5af2-87df-3fc445a60c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\,k}{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325232} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bafe0ee-1e4b-5a19-bcdb-3ff1a4d5c2ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9cbc72bf-acb2-5746-a596-97d89687c315" :question/math-academy-id "q-40762" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\frac{k}{4},\\frac{k}{2}),$ where $k$ is a positive real number. What are the polar coordinates of $P?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer where necessary." :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{k}{4})}^{2}+{(\\frac{k}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{k}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{k}^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5\\cdot \\frac{{k}^{2}}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\,k}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{(\\frac{k}{2})}{(-\\frac{k}{4})})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-2)| \\\\  & ≈1.107.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi $ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -1.107 \\\\  & ≈2.035 \\\\  & ≈2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. Therefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\,k}{4},2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8dfee2-0dcc-51dd-8359-792e8b7dcf0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324094} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc33d3f-43b1-5036-bee7-64c9f48bbe57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324353} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8acf08-5493-5784-b668-42814866416a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324861} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2836f8-1ba6-5faa-ae75-030fa6b7030c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{5},-3\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325163} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2f2323-45b4-58c8-8c06-7d06b792ddfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d8dfee2-0dcc-51dd-8359-792e8b7dcf0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324094} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ba43e9-48cb-5399-8b07-ea56e49d1700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "351973f9-e651-502b-90b1-ba68a5d4bedc" :question/math-academy-id "q-40804" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $({r}^{2},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n((\\sqrt{10})^{2},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=(10,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,-5\\sqrt{3}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de63e38-80fb-54c4-9a7c-5a96600b508f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},4\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324222} {:answer/id #uuid "23d41aa2-c3a0-5270-abba-3a1f3207c3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324266} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4dd8b9-9d94-588f-a6d0-7d91017750d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324340} {:answer/id #uuid "81916b05-53d5-5cfe-b7c6-b0642b2c06ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{3},8\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325056} {:answer/id #uuid "82355246-8fcd-5520-a5e5-83b3cb9b05c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{2},8\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23d41aa2-c3a0-5270-abba-3a1f3207c3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324266} :answer-field/id #uuid "3036c726-f5d4-51ab-a7e4-77d619c6072b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "417c4b2b-c91e-563b-b92c-41495ea7b2b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-40826" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(8,\\theta +\\pi ).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(8,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+\\pi )=(8,\\frac{9\\pi }{4})=(8,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cdd5d0b-2330-50e1-9b43-52f395035ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{63.53}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324088} {:answer/id #uuid "413df659-f577-5df6-97c8-dacdf140975c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{56.33}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324499} {:answer/id #uuid "79b7d6d5-9405-536b-a936-05c0c2af1f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35.36}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324986} {:answer/id #uuid "c9eea3b1-929c-52c6-92df-cd3e2d2cfb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{33.56}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325720} {:answer/id #uuid "e599d354-3ec8-5595-8763-23edf8265e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{53.36}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9eea3b1-929c-52c6-92df-cd3e2d2cfb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{33.56}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325720} :answer-field/id #uuid "5381d15d-5da8-5cd7-95b4-a8816b780f68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac040723-2ada-5099-94bc-dbb9317cacda" :question/math-academy-id "q-40884" :question/problem "Rounded to two decimal places, what is the acute angle between the vectors $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(1)+(0)(1)+(1)(1)+(2)(1)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{5}{6})≈{33.56}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326893 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cefc28e-3013-5543-b422-630317389bb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324744} {:answer/id #uuid "66669ffe-39e3-50ab-8627-0ea0ff13e3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324824} {:answer/id #uuid "907b1b41-6b34-5db5-8174-90d347604d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325201} {:answer/id #uuid "e2162bac-dcf4-568a-8ee7-fefc4b1a5cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325953} {:answer/id #uuid "fb17d8d2-fe6b-5b59-8da7-b2a4d7273d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cefc28e-3013-5543-b422-630317389bb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324744} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0b245ff-e662-5bc9-af4f-65d2c79b6dab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c8c8181-1284-56b6-82a4-ada1681baa78" :question/math-academy-id "q-40913" :question/problem "![](images/19/19f94b8985a4b8da7899f984e0e1c3f88668aa59531658cd0118edb35aab1107.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(-6,-2\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}}{-6}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the third quadrant. So, we find the angle in the third quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027310f9-ee0d-507a-bc28-d9da862bc3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323994} {:answer/id #uuid "13cbf72a-775b-5500-b4ce-fd7dee8abc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324150} {:answer/id #uuid "6058f5f8-765c-528f-b9e3-77d370e52e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324776} {:answer/id #uuid "df18dbbf-d1af-5d62-9ba8-07e3f6047abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325920} {:answer/id #uuid "df1f5f2f-9dda-564b-8601-3ba072795d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "027310f9-ee0d-507a-bc28-d9da862bc3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323994} :answer-field/id #uuid "58847018-c5c9-5b9a-9650-5b07e75b721b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d34cdae0-7b23-59fc-a8bc-f0d99444106f" :question/math-academy-id "q-40921" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $3a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}54 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}54 & -18 & 12 \\\\ -18 & 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}54 & -18 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n3a+\\frac{b}{c}=3\\cdot \\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{1}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326895 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10eb91a6-2d3a-53b3-b073-a10d66decb1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324127} {:answer/id #uuid "398c42e6-61f6-5edb-a6af-56df8a41a08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324439} {:answer/id #uuid "70e7b5cd-ce67-5487-8c59-0136c6664fc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324901} {:answer/id #uuid "73b39860-f650-5a09-8af5-6cb9c76b62c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324931} {:answer/id #uuid "9fba6c6f-ee94-52be-9456-59da4a7eee4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "398c42e6-61f6-5edb-a6af-56df8a41a08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324439} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b1a7497-9e7d-5e11-87dd-d6334e26484a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3df77ba-fc93-5eb8-9827-0c8cef3d9027" :question/math-academy-id "q-40923" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-10a+\\frac{6b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}90 & -30 \\\\ -30 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-27 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}90 & -30 & -27 \\\\ -30 & 10 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}90 & -30 & -27 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-10a+\\frac{6b}{c}=-10\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{10})+\\frac{6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3})}{1}=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a2e5c9-1ec3-53d7-9d65-67e652469f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324010} {:answer/id #uuid "48074d8b-44cf-5ab6-976d-e49c216938d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324554} {:answer/id #uuid "5dad58d2-2933-50ed-bf94-504e98ebf78f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324749} {:answer/id #uuid "c15de438-95e3-56a6-9a64-f99e256f9c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325633} {:answer/id #uuid "e20db4f3-7b86-5a65-991a-7ed79cec5074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dad58d2-2933-50ed-bf94-504e98ebf78f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324749} :answer-field/id #uuid "74ca8095-e929-5df1-909d-a6fa762d4c42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b6fc54b-cb36-5749-9df6-d5883bd36fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-40927" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}30 & -60 \\\\ -60 & 120\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\n Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-2a+\\frac{b}{c}=-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{2}{1}=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326897 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c8cf07-1252-5e91-b5c9-12f38c1f946f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324034} {:answer/id #uuid "91384dd4-5bcc-5860-814c-f96556460493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325204} {:answer/id #uuid "93d14029-007f-54bd-9d2a-0c82c9066807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325227} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f7e58b-5b63-5e1f-ae19-6232045969c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325419} {:answer/id #uuid "b3759c15-e036-5c30-848d-c2b42024912f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06c8cf07-1252-5e91-b5c9-12f38c1f946f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324034} :answer-field/id #uuid "6314ea6d-cf55-58df-9abc-6434d47d5c83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db567127-1e8b-50cb-a3d2-dc5e2ea462cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-40950" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,+\\,{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a-3b-6c?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}12 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(1/6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=(1/2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable.  \n\nFrom the third row, we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{3}=1,\n$$\n\nand from the first row we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n2a-3b-6c=2(\\frac{1}{2})-3(1)-6(0)=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c54f267-fd4c-5610-90e5-a07874afe3b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324082} {:answer/id #uuid "20694193-de0f-588b-9e6d-940c63acc627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324243} {:answer/id #uuid "584b8914-7f36-583d-84e3-b13e35b3b723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324705} {:answer/id #uuid "d677bf1f-1a2e-5e32-aab7-cc8c5b12bcc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325838} {:answer/id #uuid "e907eab0-0f32-5d1d-8ed1-beb5512c92bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e907eab0-0f32-5d1d-8ed1-beb5512c92bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326023} :answer-field/id #uuid "118cd9d9-245d-530a-a849-b76a205544bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b904a59c-d936-5bd3-ab5c-5deda9350aee" :question/math-academy-id "q-41626" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\\\ d\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V=\\text{Col}(A)$ spanned by the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ of the matrix $A$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ shown above. Given that ${a}_{1}⊥{a}_{2},$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: We set ${v}_{2}={a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find ${v}_{3}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{3}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{5})}{(-\\frac{1}{5})}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22258bfe-c45b-5f21-a667-30c117ad8a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324254} {:answer/id #uuid "6c19281c-1bdf-5c2c-8da6-12608411bdbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324868} {:answer/id #uuid "70f5901a-8ecc-5ed6-9e61-31029c3b52e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324902} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7069b8-37f8-51cf-aa26-912727ac8a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325421} {:answer/id #uuid "ca326694-ca81-5444-a3a9-ea0bbb188c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22258bfe-c45b-5f21-a667-30c117ad8a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324254} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1c15903-aebf-5043-94f9-7a4a1dc800d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73028088-3653-5062-a918-602555877417" :question/math-academy-id "q-41630" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B={\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\\\ d\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V=\\text{Col}(A)$ spanned by the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ of the matrix $A,$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ shown above. Given that ${a}_{1}⊥{a}_{2},$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|\\,a\\,|?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: We set ${v}_{2}={a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find ${v}_{3}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{3}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{10}{10})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-(\\frac{0}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${v}_{3}$ dividing it by $‖{v}_{3}‖=3\\sqrt{10}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $|\\,a\\,|=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326910 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13716c6d-7da7-5db2-8834-8815e837efb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324144} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8207dc-ba18-5452-aa91-bbc699e6a979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324316} {:answer/id #uuid "73fed3e7-b807-5a03-9e45-15f89f699584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324934} {:answer/id #uuid "9c1af16d-fc3c-568f-b726-4df0389c24f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5\\sqrt{2},-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325295} {:answer/id #uuid "af358834-ec05-5f7e-a913-e2746840f3be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73fed3e7-b807-5a03-9e45-15f89f699584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324934} :answer-field/id #uuid "54de1918-4006-5ab8-b837-26247c91ff0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1aff9346-7008-5138-9f81-65abfd7bc8f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-41787" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7a3c82568cfaf845708a9dcee79a3ac2e0e9d2131776fd1c7daa1c598473f3b3.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=10$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(10,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-5\\sqrt{3},-5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c20af83-4b36-5128-89ee-a2b0f277470a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324458} {:answer/id #uuid "58d3b34f-653e-5908-8110-49345a2e51c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324708} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3eb528-5cf9-526f-857f-315ec81f0eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324735} {:answer/id #uuid "b0bbe617-b13b-516f-806c-a9356a26279f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325479} {:answer/id #uuid "c5751e84-51f5-5505-bf51-a2e74963787c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0bbe617-b13b-516f-806c-a9356a26279f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325479} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac5ff18b-ee0a-5c8e-a1ac-a863dbac8c0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85d7c223-d931-53ec-98fb-80a06e5b2764" :question/math-academy-id "q-41904" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $15a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}40 & -20 \\\\ -20 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -10 & 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n15a+\\frac{b}{c}=15\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165933c2-6b08-53c0-b905-d5ec59e56a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327129} {:answer/id #uuid "e27f066c-37c1-59a9-98d6-175e81fd7e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328917} {:answer/id #uuid "f0aed3d2-6b41-5076-b071-462dc43554ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329035} {:answer/id #uuid "f3298422-dd2e-5f10-8faa-ac2572ee58dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329058} {:answer/id #uuid "f6aef26c-92d8-505d-bf2e-1e5a4b1adb2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3298422-dd2e-5f10-8faa-ac2572ee58dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329058} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae5be7f0-c577-5334-886d-25eb95c97df6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1aca67bb-ce76-56b0-b16e-e131cb0a90bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-42739" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)=t-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{(t-1)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-{t}^{2}+t)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-1+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $‖f(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{t-1}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(t-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d9790e4-77cb-5ff4-ac43-39c78331e808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327191} {:answer/id #uuid "9939c042-c8aa-5188-bbb4-1c3c3f7a7f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328257} {:answer/id #uuid "a407281c-48ae-50b3-a5ba-d986468525b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328354} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f911ba-6e39-5c15-993b-525d98cd4ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328397} {:answer/id #uuid "e51dd51b-095f-57d7-a137-944306de7d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a407281c-48ae-50b3-a5ba-d986468525b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328354} :answer-field/id #uuid "37d7ae64-5756-5393-953b-b3fac5aef0ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3eb8e400-2919-528a-91cb-5d1221ea0b97" :question/math-academy-id "q-43308" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )?$" :question/worked-solution "First, use the identity $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ) & =(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we expand the parentheses in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e102532-1da6-505c-8449-9cc76869ce8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327613} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1a0201-5d52-55b8-abe6-b78bfe3139ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327981} {:answer/id #uuid "c31eb764-054a-5f59-8e49-ecb141552eb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328639} {:answer/id #uuid "df2c1966-1b2a-5a6d-bb6c-45c4f8885c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{arcsin}(\\frac{x}{6})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328889} {:answer/id #uuid "e319cc83-daa7-5ab6-89e3-973218f6e0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c31eb764-054a-5f59-8e49-ecb141552eb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328639} :answer-field/id #uuid "381407f6-8896-57f4-8153-d40f382fa1ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03ce47aa-60a1-561f-9a2d-d11c8a6e24bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-4334" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x\\,=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/36)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/36}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{6}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{6}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=6\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (6\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0159f11a-f725-51b8-8190-72e0a3e7ae98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326948} {:answer/id #uuid "07eb3e8a-40d3-563e-a835-95d37584219c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326997} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4ecdad-1e27-598a-a760-830a37debc86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327757} {:answer/id #uuid "6c3950a1-f7ac-5fa4-96fe-05eeb381d388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The set is linearly independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327867} {:answer/id #uuid "a36eb231-0c6c-528e-9ebb-fa4f610e574c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07eb3e8a-40d3-563e-a835-95d37584219c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326997} :answer-field/id #uuid "04a5abde-4656-51fe-8f77-4902587303b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c61ad8c4-53c6-503a-a1ca-bb14fe01f90c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43790" :question/problem "Consider the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ of polynomials from $R[t],$ where\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1+t+{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1-t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2+{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{3}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}\\cdot {p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}\\cdot {p}_{3}(t)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for ${p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),$ and ${p}_{3}(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(1+t+{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(1-t)+{x}_{3}(2+{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ ({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3})+({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})t+({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-{x}_{3}.$ Substitute it into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(-{x}_{3})+2{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{3}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{3}}=\\frac{-{x}_{3}}{{x}_{3}}=-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329746 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3488da2a-e003-5561-a11e-249490760c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327389} {:answer/id #uuid "88e120f7-cc11-55d8-a9ba-c594c244f303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi +1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328111} {:answer/id #uuid "a123dead-34c9-5967-887d-230e0e578d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi +\\frac{1}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328333} {:answer/id #uuid "ac04115e-ccd2-544f-a8bf-491fb4d1e0f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328427} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f029f6-0aab-5fb5-9494-3d542b489788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3488da2a-e003-5561-a11e-249490760c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327389} :answer-field/id #uuid "84e18dde-1d57-52b3-9073-dfa4bf328a16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "559e02dd-832f-5d03-8e1c-a9c498d4da5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-43921" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\,{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{4}){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{4})-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{4}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡t=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0697fb32-13a8-5152-929c-1cbc92697a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326990} {:answer/id #uuid "29cbd9c5-1f6a-5f93-bfe0-6e1833448eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327292} {:answer/id #uuid "5f59d2b0-87ec-535c-98db-46f721956829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327771} {:answer/id #uuid "875b0e21-3bdb-537e-83a7-6070520501cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328098} {:answer/id #uuid "8c78c851-07e7-51bc-a294-83ea44fc4afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29cbd9c5-1f6a-5f93-bfe0-6e1833448eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327292} :answer-field/id #uuid "6067e08a-a253-5424-8dcb-5049856ea945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c5295f76-3e41-52a4-88d5-2769f2995c21" :question/math-academy-id "q-45875" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "As $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nLet's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/aa/aaba5af0542d29ef91d4b0e17907a81e32bccc402c48b32c205e7298630b5bc0.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ we have:\n\n- $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(-\\frac{4}{5})}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=-\\frac{4}{3}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5})}{(-\\frac{4}{5})}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{3}{4}=-\\frac{25}{12}.$"}]
